[{:db/id 17592186156927 :question/problem "Which of the following integrals has an entire integrand that is odd about zero and opposite integration limits, so the standard odd-integrand cancellation rule applies directly?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" :question/worked-solution "The direct odd-integrand cancellation rule applies to an integrable odd function on an interval with opposite endpoints: $\\int_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=0$.\n\nI satisfies this criterion: $4x^3$ is odd and continuous on $[-3,3]$, so $\\int_{-3}^{3}4x^3\\,\\mathrm{d}x=0$.\n\nII has an even, strictly positive integrand. Its value is\n\n$$\\int_{-2}^{2}(e^x+e^{-x})\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[e^x-e^{-x}]_{-2}^{2}=2(e^2-e^{-2})>0.$$\n\nIII has an odd integrand, $\\sin(2x)$, but its endpoints $-\\pi$ and $2\\pi$ are not opposites. Thus it does not satisfy the direct criterion. Nevertheless, III also has value zero because its interval spans three full periods:\n\n$$\\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi}\\sin(2x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\left[-\\frac12\\cos(2x)\\right]_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi}=-\\frac12\\cos(4\\pi)+\\frac12\\cos(-2\\pi)=0.$$\n\nTherefore, “I only” meets the stated direct odd-integrand criterion. If the question asked which integrals have value zero by any method, the answer would instead be I and III. An interval that fails the opposite-endpoint criterion can still have a zero integral by periodicity.\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
